Constructors, this, and overloading.

If encapsulation is about protecting an object during its life, a constructor is about starting that life in a valid state. It's the special method that runs when you say new, and its whole job is to take the object from just-allocated to ready-to-use.

A constructor has two tell-tale features: it has the class's name, and it has no return type. That's how Java tells it apart from an ordinary method. It runs exactly once per object, at creation. So a bank account constructor might take an owner and an opening balance, and assign them to the object's fields. Then, when you write new bank account, with "Ada" and a hundred, the constructor runs right there.

And here's the powerful part. By requiring owner and opening in the constructor, you make it impossible to create a half-built account. There's simply no way to get an object without giving it what it needs. Combine that with private final fields, and the object is valid and immutable from the moment it's born.

Now, a subtle point about the default constructor. If you write no constructor at all, Java quietly gives you a no-argument one, so that new bank account with empty parentheses works. But the moment you write any constructor of your own, that free one disappears. That catches people out — suddenly new Foo stops compiling, because you added a constructor that takes arguments.

Overloaded constructors often share setup, and rather than repeat it, one constructor can delegate to another using this, with arguments. So a constructor that takes just an owner can call this, passing the owner and a zero, delegating to the fuller constructor. The one rule is that this-call must be the very first statement. This pattern keeps a single real constructor holding all the logic, with thin convenience constructors that funnel into it.

And just like methods, constructors can be overloaded — same name, different parameter lists, and the compiler picks the right one by the arguments you pass. So you might construct an account with an owner and an opening balance, or with just an owner, which delegates to the first with a zero balance. The guideline is to offer the constructors that correspond to genuinely valid ways to create the object, and no more. If some combination of arguments would be invalid, just don't provide a constructor for it, and the type system makes that mistake impossible.

So here's the through-line. A constructor is your one guaranteed chance to establish an object's invariants. Validate the arguments there, throwing if they're wrong; assign the final fields; and then every object that exists is, by construction, a valid one. Everything the rest of the class does can safely assume it's starting from a good state.
